People hear the message about the kingdom but do not understand it. Then the devil comes. He steals what was planted in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
Others received the seed that fell in rocky places. They are the ones who hear the message and receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. So they last only for a little while. They quickly fall away from the faith when troubles or suffering come because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the message. But then, the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth choke them. They prevent it from bearing fruit.
But others received the seed that fell on good soil. They are those who hear the message and understand it. "They produce a harvest 100, 60, or 30 times more than the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. He said, "Thus is the kingdom of heaven.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The master's servants came to him. They said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did the weeds come from?"
" 'An enemy did this,' he answered. 'The servants asked, 'Do you want us to go and remove the weeds?' "
"'No,' the owner replied. 'While you are pulling up the weeds, you might pull up the wheat along with them.'"
Let them grow together until the harvest. At that time I will tell the workers what to do. This is what I will say to them. First gather the weeds. Tie them into bundles to burn. Then gather the wheat. Bring it to my storage." "The parables of the mustard seed and the yeast"
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field.
It is the smallest of your seeds. But when it grows, it is larger than all the plants of the garden. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest in its branches.
Jesus told them another parable. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast, which a woman took and mixed with three measures of flour, and it worked all through the dough."
Jesus said all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not say anything to them without a parable.
Jesus explains the parable of the tares.
Then Jesus went out from the crowd and went into the house. And his disciples came to him, saying, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man."
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the devil.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are pulled and burned in the fire. So it will be on the day of reckoning.
The Son of Man will send his angels. They will uproot from his kingdom all that causes sin. They will save from all who do evil.
They will throw them in the steeds, the mortgaged ones. In them, they will weep and strike the earth with their feet.
Then the people of God will shine forth like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. And whoever has ears to hear, let him hear. A tale of the secret treasures and the pearl.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field, which when a man found it, he hid it again. And he was exceedingly glad. Then he went and sold all that he had, and bought that field.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking a fine pearl.
He found a pearl of great value. So he went and sold all he had. And he bought the pearl. The story of the net.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. That was cast into the lake. And it gathered all kinds of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen drew the net to shore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they cast the bad fish away.
Thus will the Day of Judgment be. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
They will meet the villains in the beautiful rampage. In it, the villains cry and gnash their teeth.
Do you understand all these things? asked Jesus. Yes, they answered.
He said to them: "Every scribe who is a trained observer of the kingdoms of the heavens, is like a master of a household. He brings out of his storeroom new and old alike." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. And there he began to teach the people in their temple. And they were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get this power to work these miracles?" they asked.
Is not this the carpenter's son? Is not his mother called Mary? And are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
What was all that, sir? All, all, all, all his sisters are with us? So from where does he have these things?
They were not pleased with him at all. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country, and in his own house."
He did few miracles there because of their lack of faith.
John the Baptist's head is cut off.
In those days, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has such power to perform miracles."
Herod had arrested John, bound him, and thrown him into prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John was telling Herod, "It is unlawful for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
So he promised her with a promise that he would give her whatever she asked for.
Herodias's daughter said to her mother, "What shall I ask for?" The girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very angry. But he remembered a promise he had made to himself and his dinner guests. So he asked one of his men to give her what she had asked for.
Herod cut off John's head in prison.
They brought his head on a large plate and took it to the girl. After that, they put it with her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowd heard about this. So they followed him on foot from the villages.
When Jesus came to the land, he saw a large crowd. He was moved with compassion for them, and he healed their sick.
As evening approached, the disciples came to him. They said, "There is nothing here." "It is late. Send the crowds away. They can go to the villages and buy themselves something to eat."
Jesus answered, "There is no need for them to go. Give them something to eat."
"We have only five loaves of bread and two fish," they answered.
"Have them brought to me," he said.
Then Jesus directed the people to sit down on the grass. And He took the five loaves and the two fish, and looking up to heaven, He blessed and broke the loaves and He gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the people.
