After dismissing them, he went up alone to the hill of Jabalani to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was already far from land. It was being rocked by waves because the wind was blowing against it.
Very early in the morning Jesus went out to the disciples. And he went along the Sea of Galilee.
They saw him walking on the lake and were frightened. They said, "This is a ghost!" and cried out in fear.
Immediately Jesus sent them to the nearby villages, saying, "Go! Tell everyone what great things I have done for you, because I am the Lord your Savior."
"Lord, if it is you," Peter asked. "Tell me to come to you on the water."
"Come," said Jesus. So Peter got out of the boat. He walked towards Jesus on the water.
But Peter, when he saw the wind, was afraid. And beginning to sink, he cried out, saying, "Lord, save me!"
Jesus immediately seized him and said, "You of little faith! Why did you doubt?"
When they went up into the ship, the cold lessened.
Then those who were in the ship began to prostrate themselves, bending down, and saying, 'Glory be to our Lord! Indeed, His promise is ever established.' Or have We not drowned the generations before them, for that they were rebellious against the signs of the Lord? And they were not believers.
They crossed the lake and reached Galilee.
The men who used to live there knew Jesus. So they sent a message all over the nearby country. The people brought all their sick to Jesus.
They asked him to allow only the sick to touch the edge of his robe. And he healed all who touched him.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked,
Why don't your students obey what the adults teach them? Your students don't wash their hands before they eat.
Jesus answered, "Why do you not obey God's command? Instead, you want to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother" (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16). He also said, " 'Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be put to death.' " (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to tell their parents: "The giving that you may receive from us is a gift dedicated to God."
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You make the word of God void for the sake of your traditions.
O hypocrites! Isaiah indeed spoke the truth when he prophesied about you. He said:
"These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me."
Their worship means nothing to me. It does not teach us anything but human rules." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Himself. He said, "Hear and understand."
What enters into the mouth does not defile you, but what comes out of the mouth, that defiles you.
Then the disciples came to him. And they said, "Do you not understand that the Pharisees were offended when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are some plants that my Father in heaven did not plant; these will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
What is the matter with you? Why do you believe in some of the Scripture and disbelieve in some? Jesus asked them.
Didn't you see? Everything that goes into the mouth goes into the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and other sexual sins. Also theft, false testimony, and lying to others.
This is what makes a person unclean. But eating without washing hands does not make a person unclean. The faith of a Canaanite woman
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A woman from Canaan lived near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon has seized my daughter. She suffers greatly."
Jesus did not say a word. His disciples came to him. And they asked him: "Send her away from us. She is crying out behind us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Master! Help me!" she said.
He answered, "It is not right that we should take the children's bread and cast it unto the dogs."
"Yes, Lord," she said. "But even the dogs eat from the crumbs of their masters' tables."
Jesus said: "Woman, you were hoping for a better outcome! What you are seeking will be given to you." And her daughter was healed at that very hour. Jesus feeds four thousand people.
Jesus left from there. He walked along the shore of the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up on a mountainside and sat down.
A large crowd of people came to him. They brought with them the blind, those who could not walk, and also the sick, those who could not speak, and many others. And they placed them at his feet, and he rose up and healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak were speaking. The paralyzed were healed. Those who could not walk were walking. The blind could see. So the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus turned his speech to his disciples and said, "I am sorry for these people, for they have stayed with me for three days and have no food. I do not want to send them away hungry, lest they become too weak to walk to their homes."
His disciples answered him. "There is nothing here," they said. "Where can we find enough bread to feed this large crowd?"
How much bread do you have?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they replied, "and a few small fish."
Jesus said to the crowds, "Sit on the ground."
He took the seven loaves and the fish, and gave thanks to God. Then he broke them and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples took seven baskets of leftover fragments.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowd away, he went up into the boat. Then he went into the region near Magadan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He replied: "In the evening you look at the sky. You say: The weather will be fine. The sky is red."
And in the morning she says: "Today it will rain. The sky is red and overcast." She knows what she sees in the sky means. But you do not understand the signs of what is happening now.
The disbelieving people are searching for a strange sign. But only Jonah's sign will be given to them. Then Jesus turned away from them and departed. The plot of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed to the other side of the lake. They forgot to take bread.
"Beware," Jesus said to them. "Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked among themselves about this. And they said: "He must be saying this because we did not take bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said to them, "Why do you worry, saying, 'We lack the things we need'?"
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you remember the five loaves of bread for the 5000? Don't you remember the number of baskets of leftovers you collected?
What do you remember about the seven loaves for the four thousand? What do you remember about how many baskets of pieces did you collect?
How is it that you do not understand? I was not talking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then, suddenly, the disciples understood that Jesus had not told them to beware of the leaven of bread. Rather, He had warned them about what was being taught by the Pharisees and Sadducees. In the way Peter had, that Jesus is the Messiah.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. And he asked his disciples there, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "Some say it is John the Baptist; others say it is Elijah; and still others say it is Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
But what about you? he asked. Who do you say that I am?
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Mr. James son of Zebedee! This cannot be taught to you except by someone from heaven."
Here is what I say to you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus revealed to his disciples that they should not tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about his coming death.
From that time on, Jesus began to teach his disciples what would happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. There he must suffer many things at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed, and on the third day he must rise from the dead.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. For you are not thinking about the things of God, but about the things of people."
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "If anyone wants to come after me, he must deny himself and take up his cross and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What profit is it to a man if he gains the whole world and loses his own soul? Or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father, and with Him will come His angels. And He will reward each one according to his works.
I am not able to tell you, I can only speak the truth. Some of those standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his Kingdom."
The appearance of Jesus changes his form.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John, the brother of James, and led them up a high mountain. They were all alone.
There before them his appearance was transformed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three shelters. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And as Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. And a voice came from the cloud, saying, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased; listen to him!"
And when the disciples heard this, they were deeply sorrowful. And as they came, their faces were cast down.
But Jesus came and touched them. And He said to them, "Arise. Do not be afraid."
When they raised their eyes to the sky, they saw no one except Jesus.
They went down the mountain. On the way down, Jesus told them what to do. "Do not tell anyone what you have seen," he said. "Wait until the Son of Man is raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "Thus it is written. The prophet Elijah is destined to return and renew all things."
But I tell you, Elijah has come, and they did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will make the Son of Man suffer.
Then the disciples realized that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus feeds a boy from whom a demon had been cast out.
As they came near the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus; he knelt down before him.
"Oh, my lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He is trembling violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him for your followers. But they could not heal him.
"O ye of little faith! How long shall I bear with you?"
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. He was healed at that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. And they said to Him, "Why could we not cast out that demon?"
He answered, "Because you have so little faith. I tell you the truth, if you have faith as big as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea,' and it will be done. You may ask for anything in my name, and I will do it. What is impossible for man is possible for God."
